    "output_tokens": 671,
    "wall_seconds": 6.054596900939941,
    "final_answer": "**(E)**\n\nThe principle states only a sufficient condition for labeling (if most consumers would be upset, then label). The application treats the failure of that condition (most consumers would not care) as establishing that labeling is *not* required \u2014 but the principle never says the condition is necessary for labeling. This is the classic error described in (E): confusing \"labeling is required under condition X\" with \"labeling is not required when X is absent\" (denying the antecedent).\n\n- (A), (B): Irrelevant \u2014 the principle concerns consumers of that product, and safety is not part of the principle's logic.\n- (C): No incompatible value judgment is used.\n- (D): The principle concerns being upset upon discovery, not purchasing behavior.",
    "grader_detail": "expected=E, got=E"
  },
