    "final_answer": "**B**\n\nThe antidilution provision makes sense only if dilution doesn't actually reduce environmental harm. Option (B) provides exactly this justification: since diluted XTX in sufficient quantities is just as harmful as the concentrated form, allowing dilution would let manufacturers dump the same total amount of toxic chemical into the environment while technically complying with the 500 ppm limit\u2014defeating the law's purpose of reducing harm. \n\nThe other options fail: (A) concerns risks of improper incineration and arguably undermines the provision; (C) suggests dumping is ultimately harmless, weakening the law's rationale; (D) concerns dump owners' acceptance thresholds, not environmental harm; and (E) suggests manufacturers have no cost incentive to dilute anyway.",
